# OtoDock Configuration
# Copy to config.env and edit. Secrets auto-generate on first run if omitted.
# This file is the single source of truth for all services (proxy, phone, Docker MCPs).

# ── Auto-generated secrets (leave blank for auto-generation on first run) ──
# A value you set yourself needs at least 32 characters (openssl rand -base64 48):
# a new shorter JWT_SECRET is refused at boot, one already in use only warns.
PROXY_API_KEY=
JWT_SECRET=
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ── Credential encryption key (optional) ──
# Stored secrets (MCP and engine credentials, 2FA, SMTP) are encrypted with a
# key derived from JWT_SECRET unless this is set. Set, new secrets are written
# with it and older ones still open. Comma-separated: the first key encrypts,
# the others only decrypt (rotation). 32 characters or more. Rolling back to
# 1.7.0 after setting it loses the secrets written under the other key.
# CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── Database ──
# `proxy/setup.sh` auto-generates a strong POSTGRES_PASSWORD + matching
# DATABASE_URL into config.env on a FRESH install, and the bundled `postgres`
# service reads POSTGRES_PASSWORD via `docker compose --env-file config.env`
# (it refuses to start with a default password). Leave both blank to let
# setup.sh handle it; set them explicitly for a managed/remote database. A
# fully-dockerized proxy points the host at `otodock-postgres:5432` (compose
# network) instead of localhost.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://otodock:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/otodock
# The bundled Postgres runs a SEPARATE superuser `otodock_admin` (used by the
# one-shot `otodock-db-init` and by scripts/restore.sh, both over the
# container's local socket); the app connects as the non-superuser role
# `otodock`, so a leaked app password is not superuser. Optional: unset, the
# superuser has no network password and is reachable only through
#   docker compose exec otodock-postgres psql -U otodock_admin -d otodock
# set, it is applied on every start (TCP admin tools such as pgAdmin), and
# unsetting it removes the password again on the next start.
# OTODOCK_DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Seconds the one-shot DB init waits for Postgres TCP on boot (covers a large
# database's WAL replay before it accepts connections).
# OTODOCK_DB_INIT_WAIT_S=180

# ── Server ──
# Bare-metal: the port the proxy binds. Docker (T2): the published HOST port —
# the container side stays 8400 (pinned in docker-compose.yml), so e.g.
# PROXY_PORT=8410 serves the same stack on :8410 without touching the image.
PROXY_PORT=8400
# Docker (T2): the host interface the published port binds to. Empty (default)
# publishes on all interfaces, as before. Behind a same-host reverse proxy set
# PROXY_BIND_IP=127.0.0.1 (with TRUSTED_PROXY below) so only the edge can reach
# the proxy and X-Forwarded-For cannot be forged from another interface / IPv6 /
# a hairpinning sidecar (the installation guide's HTTPS section names both).
# PROXY_BIND_IP=127.0.0.1
# Docker (T2): the release the stack runs, the image tag of every OtoDock
# service. Unset, the compose files run the release they shipped with.
# scripts/upgrade.sh sets it together with that release's compose files: a new
# value alone keeps the old compose files, which is not an upgrade.
# OTODOCK_VERSION=
# Server timezone (IANA, e.g. Europe/Athens). On the FIRST install the platform
# timezone defaults to this server's local time (task scheduling / cron / agent
# datetime), instead of UTC — set once, never overwritten by updates or an
# admin's later change. Bare-metal reads /etc/localtime automatically; a Docker
# proxy is UTC unless you set TZ here (the compose passes it into the container).
# TZ=Europe/Athens
# Public URL of the dashboard. Also decides the session-cookie `Secure` flag:
# an https:// URL → Secure cookies (correct, incl. behind a TLS-terminating
# reverse proxy); an http:// URL (localhost/LAN, pre-TLS) → NON-Secure so the
# browser actually keeps the login cookie (a Secure cookie is dropped over http,
# which makes login appear to work then 401 everywhere). Override with
# COOKIE_SECURE=true/false if your edge differs from this URL's scheme.
# The proxy serves the dashboard itself — same host/port as the API (keep the
# port in sync with PROXY_PORT above); set your real public URL when fronted
# by a reverse proxy / tunnel (e.g. https://otodock.example.com).
# Changing this on a RUNNING install: re-run `docker compose up -d` (or
# scripts/compose.sh up -d) so Collabora is RECREATED — it bakes this URL
# into its iframe-embedding config at container-create; a restart alone
# keeps the old URL and document previews silently fail in the browser.
DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8400
# COOKIE_SECURE=
# Behind a reverse proxy: the address the proxy connects FROM (IPs or CIDRs,
# comma-separated). OtoDock then reads the real client address from
# X-Forwarded-For, and only from these hops. Wrong or unset behind a proxy,
# every visitor shares the proxy's address: ten failed logins from anyone
# lock everybody out, and the local-network-only restriction cannot tell
# people apart. The value per layout:
#   bare metal, reverse proxy on the same host: nothing to set (an empty
#     value trusts 127.0.0.1);
#   Docker (T2), reverse proxy on the same host: the otodock bridge gateway,
#     10.200.0.1 unless you changed the subnet (docker network inspect
#     otodock -f '{{(index .IPAM.Config 0).Gateway}}'), with the proxy port
#     published on 127.0.0.1 only;
#   a reverse proxy container on the otodock network: pin its address
#     (ipv4_address in your compose override) and give that one address,
#     never the whole subnet;
#   a reverse proxy on another machine: that machine's IP.
# The reverse proxy must append the client to X-Forwarded-For (nginx:
# proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;). Check it:
# after ten failed logins from one outside address (a phone on mobile data),
# a login from another address still works. Forwarding headers that arrive
# from an address not listed here are ignored, a local-network-only account
# is refused through them, the proxy logs one ERROR per address per hour
# naming it, and Admin, Setup, Security and a banner on every admin page and
# chat show the same warning. On bare metal an empty value trusts 127.0.0.1;
# inside a container nothing is trusted until you set this: with an https
# DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL and nothing set here, the boot logs an ERROR and the
# banner names the address to add. The proxy's access log shows the resolved
# client address.
# TRUSTED_PROXY=
#
# Docker (T2) networks. The proxy alone bridges three internal (no-egress)
# networks so the control planes and the untrusted sidecars can't reach each
# other or the wider network: socketproxy (the Docker socket proxy),
# otodock-data (Postgres) and otodock-internal (file-tools + Collabora). Each is
# a pinned /24 inside 10/8, disjoint from the shared bridge (10.200.0.0/24) and
# the MCP pool (10.201.0.0/16). Override only if a second install shares this
# Docker daemon or a subnet overlaps your LAN.
# OTODOCK_SOCKETPROXY_SUBNET=10.202.0.0/24
# OTODOCK_DATA_SUBNET=10.203.0.0/24
# OTODOCK_INTERNAL_SUBNET=10.204.0.0/24
VAPID_EMAIL=admin@localhost

# ── OtoDock relay (hosted native push, hosted OAuth, license activation) ──
# A connected install reaches the live OtoDock relay by DEFAULT (no config) —
# that's how native mobile push works with ZERO Firebase server credentials on
# your side: the relay holds OtoDock's FCM service account. Two opt-outs:
#   OTODOCK_AIR_GAPPED=true     # make this install reach OtoDock NOT AT ALL
#   OTODOCK_RELAY_BASE=...      # override endpoint (staging / private relay)
# BYO-Firebase escape hatch (advanced) — send native push from your OWN Firebase
# project instead of the relay (rebuild the app w/ your own google-services.json):
#   FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PATH=/path/to/your-fcm-service-account.json

# ── Managed / OtoDock-cloud installs (operator-only — leave unset to self-host) ──
# OTODOCK_CLOUD=true marks an OtoDock-operated install (the control plane manages
# connectivity + licensing). Self-hosters leave it unset.
#   OTODOCK_CLOUD=true
# OTODOCK_FORCED_SETTINGS pins platform_settings the customer-admin must NOT change:
# a JSON object {key: value}. The forced value wins on every read, writes to it are
# ignored, and the admin UI hides/locks it. Bools may be JSON true/false (stored as
# "1"/""). Fail-safe: blank / invalid JSON / non-object → ignored. Do NOT include
# license_key here (use OTODOCK_LICENSE_KEY — forcing it would freeze relay re-issue).
#   OTODOCK_FORCED_SETTINGS={"session_retention_enabled":true,"session_retention_days":"180","smtp_host":"mail.otodock.io","smtp_port":"587","password_min_score":"3"}
# OTODOCK_LICENSE_KEY seeds platform_settings.license_key ONCE at startup when the DB
# has none (bootstrap on container rebuild). The license_check_worker then owns updates
# via the relay re-issue — this is a seed, never an override.
#   OTODOCK_LICENSE_KEY=otolic_...
# OTODOCK_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY / OTODOCK_TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY — Cloudflare Turnstile login
# bot-protection. When BOTH are set they take precedence over the DB and the admin sees
# only a read-only "managed" badge (keys never exposed, admin writes ignored). Use these
# dedicated vars (NOT OTODOCK_FORCED_SETTINGS, which would show the value) to keep the
# secret hidden. Self-hosters leave these blank and set their own keys in Setup → Security.
#   OTODOCK_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=0x4AAAAAAA...
#   OTODOCK_TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=0x4AAAAAAA...

# ── Sandbox network isolation (ALWAYS ON — no toggle) ──
# Every LOCAL agent sandbox (CLI / Codex / Direct-LLM stdio MCPs) is wrapped in a
# pasta network namespace: NAT'd outbound internet only, with RFC1918 + the
# host's own subnet + cloud-metadata blackholed at the kernel level. Egress is
# carved ONLY to the proxy hook port + each session's configured MCP / docker-MCP
# / local-LLM targets (auto-derived). Requires `passt` (pasta) + `iproute2` +
# unprivileged user namespaces — the proxy HARD-FAILS startup if they're missing
# (the installer sets them up). There is NO un-isolated mode: for genuinely
# un-isolated execution, use a remote machine. Satellites are unaffected (no
# bwrap there).

# ── Phone Server (optional — only needed if phone features are used) ──
PHONE_SERVER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9093
# Plain http only to this machine or a private network (or a single-label Docker
# name): an http URL to a public address gets nothing sent (502, one ERROR an
# hour); use https across the internet. The phone daemon refuses the same for
# its PROXY_URL.
# The address your PBX dials to reach the phone daemon (AudioSocket :9092 +
# register API :9093) — baked into the generated dialplan snippet. Auto-detected
# from the default-route interface, which is correct on bare metal but WRONG
# inside Docker (it picks the unreachable container-internal IP): in the
# containerised deployment set this to the Docker HOST's LAN IP.
# OTO_AUDIOSOCKET_PUBLIC_HOST=192.168.1.10
# Docker (T2): the host address the phone daemon's AudioSocket (:9092) and
# register API (:9093) are published on. Unset, the ports listen on
# OTO_AUDIOSOCKET_PUBLIC_HOST (the address the PBX already dials), or on
# 127.0.0.1 when that is unset too (only this host reaches them). Set it to a
# LAN IP when the public host is a hostname (docker compose then refuses the
# whole file with "invalid IP address" and no service starts) or an address
# this host does not own (the phone container fails to start).
# Never 0.0.0.0 on an internet-facing host: AudioSocket has no
# authentication of its own.
# OTO_PHONE_BIND=
# Calls agents may place through the platform (per agent and per tied user
# each hour, per agent each day; 0 = no cap), and optional E.164 prefixes a
# number must start with (comma-separated, for example +30,+357; empty = any
# number).
# PHONE_CALLS_PER_AGENT_PER_HOUR=10
# PHONE_CALLS_PER_USER_PER_HOUR=5
# PHONE_CALLS_PER_AGENT_PER_DAY=50
# PHONE_CALL_ALLOWED_PREFIXES=
# DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=
# CARTESIA_API_KEY=

# ── Audio/Video playback (optional) ──
# ffmpeg/ffprobe transcode non-web-native media (e.g. iPhone HEVC) so audio and
# video play inline in chat + workspace. PROXY-SIDE ONLY — never exposed to the
# agent sandbox or satellites. Leave blank to use the host PATH (dev). In
# production point these at a path OUTSIDE the sandbox-mounted system dirs
# (/usr,/bin,/lib,/sbin) so a sandboxed process can't even see the binary:
#   OTO_FFMPEG_PATH=/opt/otodock/bin/ffmpeg
#   OTO_FFPROBE_PATH=/opt/otodock/bin/ffprobe
# OTO_FFMPEG_PATH=
# OTO_FFPROBE_PATH=
# Transcode/remux cache (default: <PLATFORM_DATA_DIR>/media-cache).
# OTO_MEDIA_CACHE_DIR=

# ── SSO / OIDC (optional — enables single sign-on) ──
# Works with Authentik, Keycloak, Authelia, oauth2-proxy, Cloudflare Access, etc.
# Discovery URL is fetched at startup; the four endpoint URLs auto-derive.
# OIDC_REDIRECT_URI auto-derives to ${DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL}/auth/callback.
# OIDC_ENABLED=true
# OIDC_PROVIDER_NAME=SSO                                    # button label: "Sign in with <name>"
# OIDC_DISCOVERY_URL=https://auth.example.com/application/o/<app-slug>/.well-known/openid-configuration
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Role group mapping — name your provider's groups and reference them here.
# Missing env vars = that role isn't mapped (group membership is ignored).
# The mapping is also the access gate: a user in NONE of the mapped groups
# cannot sign in over SSO — configure at least one group.
# OIDC_ROLE_ADMIN_GROUP=OtoDock Admin
# OIDC_ROLE_CREATOR_GROUP=OtoDock Creator
# OIDC_ROLE_MEMBER_GROUP=OtoDock Member
# Advanced: override individual endpoints if your provider doesn't expose
# discovery, or to pin a specific URL:
# OIDC_AUTHORIZE_URL=    OIDC_TOKEN_URL=    OIDC_USERINFO_URL=    OIDC_LOGOUT_URL=
# The sign-in verifies the ID token's signature and issuer: discovery names
# both; without discovery they come from the token's issuer when it is on the
# token endpoint's host, or set them here.
# OIDC_ISSUER=    OIDC_JWKS_URL=
# OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile email groups                    # openid is always added
# OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=                                        # override auto-derive
# Making an external link from a provider account is a plain login round trip
# (the provider says it is the same account). True asks for a new login there
# (prompt=login) and refuses an old one — only for a provider whose flows
# honour that; Authentik's defaults do not.
# OIDC_CONFIRM_FRESH_LOGIN=false
# OIDC_CONFIRM_REQUIRE_AUTH_TIME=false                      # with the above: refuse an ID token without auth_time

# ── Collabora Online (document editing/preview) ──
#
# OSS DEFAULT — sub-path mode. Just set DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL above and
# everything else auto-derives:
#   COLLABORA_URL              = ${DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL}/collabora
#   COLLABORA_FRAME_ANCESTORS  = host of DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL
#   COLLABORA_SERVICE_ROOT     = /collabora
#   COLLABORA_BACKEND_URL      = http://localhost:9981  (compose: http://collabora:9980)
# The platform proxy reverse-proxies /collabora/* to the Collabora container
# internally — your reverse proxy / Cloudflare Tunnel only needs the dashboard
# upstream, no per-route blocks. Document preview works out of the box.
#
# To override (only if you need a non-default deployment shape):
#
#   Subdomain mode (Collabora on a separate FQDN, e.g. behind a different auth):
#     COLLABORA_URL=https://collabora.example.com
#     # COLLABORA_FRAME_ANCESTORS auto-derived from DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL host
#
#   Cloud / OtoDock-hosted central Collabora:
#     COLLABORA_URL=https://collabora.otodock.io
#     # WOPI_BASE_URL must be publicly reachable from central Collabora
#
# COLLABORA_URL=
# COLLABORA_FRAME_ANCESTORS=

# WOPI_BASE_URL = the URL Collabora uses to call back into the proxy to fetch
# and save files. Must be reachable from the Collabora container (often the
# proxy's LAN IP + port, not the public hostname). Defaults to http://127.0.0.1:{PROXY_PORT}.
# WOPI_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.10:8400

# COLLABORA_BACKEND_URL = where the platform proxy reaches Collabora internally.
# Default: http://localhost:9981 (native install / dev). Compose deployments
# should set http://collabora:9980 (service DNS).
# COLLABORA_BACKEND_URL=http://localhost:9981

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Turn ceilings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How long a chat turn may stream nothing with no tool running before it ends
# with the "silent" card (seconds, at least 60). A Claude Code or Codex chat on
# a slow local model that thinks longer than 10 minutes before its first word
# needs a higher value; a Direct LLM chat has no silence ceiling.
# TURN_SILENCE_S=600

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session concurrency / memory admission (small-host tuning)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# New local sessions pass
# two gates: a reservation budget (Gate 1) and a live free-RAM veto (Gate 2,
# reclaimable-aware, sidecar-aware). Denials name their real cause ("busy" =
# sessions hold the box; "host memory" = something else eats the RAM), idle
# sessions are auto-evicted to make room when session pressure is the cause,
# and an EMPTY platform always admits its first session (swap-backed) rather
# than hard-locking. Defaults suit 8GB+; on a 4-8GB VM you mostly leave these
# alone and let eviction/veto do their job — sizing intuition: a CLI session
# reserves 850MB, a Direct-LLM session 250MB, so 4GB fits about 1-2 heavy or
# 3-4 light sessions alongside postgres/collabora/MCP sidecars.
#
# Per-session memory reserve estimates (MB). Lower HEAVY below measured RSS
# only if you accept packing tighter than the real footprint.
# SESSION_EST_HEAVY_MB=850
# SESSION_EST_LIGHT_MB=250
#
# Gate-2 swap credit (MB): live free-RAM gains min(SwapFree/2, this) so a
# small box WITH swap packs one more session by paging cold heap. 0 disables.
# SESSION_SWAP_CREDIT_MB=512
#
# Gate-1 budget fraction of the proxy's memory total (generous by design --
# Gate 2 is the real safety).
# BUDGET_FRACTION=0.90
#
# Gate-2 floor: live free RAM (MB) that must REMAIN after admitting. Effective
# floor = max(this, 3% of total).
# SESSION_RESERVE_FLOOR_MB=256
#
# Minimum idle age (seconds) before a session may be evicted to admit another.
# SESSION_EVICT_FLOOR_S=300
#
# Optional hard cap on concurrent local sessions (0 = off). An operator escape
# hatch -- the memory gates are the intended control.
# OTODOCK_MAX_LOCAL_SESSIONS=0
#
# Sessions one person may hold at once (0 = no cap), session starts allowed
# at once (0 = half the CPUs, at least 2), how often the session index is
# written, how long a session start waits for a free slot, and how long a
# parked delegate result or continuation wake waits for one before it is
# stored for the chat's next turn (seconds).
# OTODOCK_MAX_SESSIONS_PER_USER=8
# SESSION_SPAWN_CONCURRENCY=0
# SESSION_INDEX_FLUSH_S=2.0
# ADMISSION_QUEUE_WAIT_S=30
# ADMISSION_WAKE_WAIT_S=600
#
# Scheduled tasks: active clocked tasks one person may create and one agent
# may hold (admins are exempt), and pending self-continuations per chat and
# per agent (0 = no cap).
# TASK_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_USER=25
# TASK_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_AGENT=100
# CONTINUATION_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_CHAT=10
# CONTINUATION_MAX_ACTIVE_PER_AGENT=100
#
# T2 (docker-compose) only: proxy container memory cap. Default 0 = uncapped
# (deliberate -- Gate 2 reads host MemAvailable and sessions may lean on swap).
# Set e.g. 12g for hard OOM containment of the proxy tree; the sidecar-leak
# veto stays active either way (capped Gate 2 = min(cgroup, host)).
# OTODOCK_PROXY_MEM_LIMIT=0
#
# T2 (docker-compose) only: a PID ceiling for the whole proxy container, so a
# fork bomb in one sandbox can't exhaust the host PID space. A container-wide
# FLOOR sized well above the platform's real thread/process count at full
# session load; per-session containment is separate. 0 = unlimited.
# OTODOCK_PROXY_PIDS_LIMIT=32768
#
# Default memory cap injected into docker-MCP sidecar containers whose compose
# declares no limit of its own (an MCP's explicit mem_limit always wins; a
# json-file log-rotation default rides along the same way). Applied on T1 via
# the generated compose override and on T2 via the pull rewrite, with
# memswap_limit = mem_limit. Set 0/none to disable injection.
# OTODOCK_MCP_DEFAULT_MEM_LIMIT=2g
#
# T2 (docker-compose) only: file-tools container memory cap (memswap = mem, no
# swap growth). Above the sidecar default because file-tools also runs
# LibreOffice conversions and image editing; its parse workers self-limit
# below this cap and fail a single call cleanly first. Raise it if you parse
# very heavy documents; 0 disables.
# OTODOCK_FILETOOLS_MEM_LIMIT=4g
#
# Universal per-file size cap (MB) — ONE knob for the satellite sync engine
# (manifest + push/pull) AND every dashboard/chat upload. Files above it never
# sync to machines and are rejected at upload. Satellites learn the value in
# the auth handshake; machines below satellite 0.5.103 stay at the legacy
# 100 MB regardless. The global request-body backstop follows this cap
# (override with MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES if needed).
# OTODOCK_MAX_FILE_MB=1024
#
# Chunk size (MB) for large dashboard uploads: files bigger than one chunk
# upload as N sequential requests of at most this size, so CDN/gateway
# request-body caps (Cloudflare ~100 MB, nginx client_max_body_size) never see
# the whole file. The server dictates this to clients at upload start. Keep it
# under your edge's per-request cap; the default clears common setups with
# margin.
# OTODOCK_UPLOAD_CHUNK_MB=32
#
# Large uploads in progress: open at once per person and in total (0 = no
# cap), MB a person may have staged (default twice OTODOCK_MAX_FILE_MB), and
# how long a started upload may wait for its first chunk before it is
# dropped (seconds; one with chunks keeps 24 hours to resume). Every upload
# write (a new upload, each chunk, the final copy) is refused when the data
# disk would keep less free space than the larger of the two floors.
# OTODOCK_UPLOAD_MAX_OPEN=4
# OTODOCK_UPLOAD_MAX_OPEN_TOTAL=256
# OTODOCK_UPLOAD_STAGING_USER_MB=2048
# OTODOCK_UPLOAD_FIRST_CHUNK_S=3600
# OTODOCK_MIN_FREE_DISK_MB=5120
# OTODOCK_MIN_FREE_DISK_PCT=5
#
# Request body caps (bytes): the login routes, webhook receivers, requests
# with no valid credential (a dashboard session cookie, the service key or a
# session token as the bearer, or a WOPI access token on the WOPI and
# Collabora routes), and every other route except uploads and the other
# large-file routes (those keep the MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES backstop).
# 0 turns a tier off.
# MAX_AUTH_BODY_BYTES=65536
# MAX_WEBHOOK_BODY_BYTES=2097152
# MAX_UNAUTH_BODY_BYTES=65536
# MAX_JSON_BODY_BYTES=8388608
#
# Webhooks: a sender that has been checked may send more. A signed delivery
# from GitHub (GitLab, Jira) and a trigger fire whose API key verified, up to
# these (bytes; never below MAX_WEBHOOK_BODY_BYTES). Bodies read at once are
# bounded in bytes, in all and per client address (0 turns a bound off).
# MAX_WEBHOOK_SIGNED_BODY_BYTES=26214400
# MAX_WEBHOOK_KEYED_BODY_BYTES=26214400
# MAX_WEBHOOK_INFLIGHT_BYTES=134217728
# MAX_WEBHOOK_INFLIGHT_BYTES_PER_CLIENT=33554432
#
# Text files larger than this are downloaded, not shown inline (bytes); zip
# downloads built at once, paths per request, files per archive and total
# input size (MB); files and total size (MB) one copy request may copy;
# temporary public image links: size of one image (MB),
# images per session, all of them together (MB).
# INLINE_TEXT_MAX_BYTES=5242880
# ZIP_MAX_CONCURRENT=2
# ZIP_MAX_PATHS=500
# ZIP_MAX_ENTRIES=100000
# ZIP_MAX_INPUT_MB=4096
# COPY_MAX_ENTRIES=100000
# COPY_MAX_INPUT_MB=4096
# TEMP_IMAGE_MAX_MB=20
# TEMP_IMAGE_MAX_PER_SESSION=20
# TEMP_IMAGE_MAX_TOTAL_MB=256
#
# Password hashes computed at once and how many sign-ins may wait for a
# turn before the rest are refused with a retry; webhook key checks at once
# plus how many may wait before further webhook calls are refused.
# AUTH_HASH_CONCURRENCY=4
# AUTH_HASH_MAX_WAITERS=64
# WEBHOOK_KEY_VERIFY_CONCURRENCY=2
# WEBHOOK_KEY_VERIFY_MAX_WAITERS=16
#
# A remote machine's file-change events: handled in flight per machine
# (more are dropped with a warning), running at once per machine and in
# total, and the size of the last-change map; threads that commit uploaded
# and pulled files to disk.
# SAT_FILE_CHANGED_MAX_INFLIGHT_PER_MACHINE=64
# SAT_FILE_CHANGED_CONCURRENCY_PER_MACHINE=4
# SAT_FILE_CHANGED_CONCURRENCY_GLOBAL=8
# SAT_FILE_CHANGED_STAMP_MAX=4096
# FILE_COMMIT_WORKERS=4
#
# Database pools and the event loop (defaults suit about 100 concurrent
# sessions; one proxy holds at most shared + fast + bulk + 2 + 3 connections (52),
# Postgres allows 100). Timeouts in seconds; 0 disables the two server-side
# timeouts, the liveness probe, the connection limit and the short-stall
# report.
# DB_POOL_MAX_SIZE=30
# DB_POOL_TIMEOUT_S=10
# DB_FAST_LANE_WORKERS=6
# DB_BULK_LANE_WORKERS=10
# DB_LOOP_POOL_TIMEOUT_S=0.5
# DB_LOOP_BREAKER_S=5
# DB_LOOP_LIVENESS_PROBE_S=1.0
# DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_S=300
# DB_IDLE_IN_TX_TIMEOUT_S=60
# DEFAULT_EXECUTOR_WORKERS=32
# HTTP_LIMIT_CONCURRENCY=4000
# WS_WRITE_BUFFER_MAX_BYTES=4194304
# WS_WRITE_STALL_S=60
# BULK_CREDIT_BYTES=1048576
# PUSH_STALL_S=90
# PULL_STALL_S=90
# LOOP_WATCHDOG_REPORT_S=0.25
#
# Recover-bin capture cap (MB): files LARGER than this are deleted/overwritten
# WITHOUT an undo copy (Windows-Recycle-Bin-style — big media bypasses the bin
# so a couple of video-sized deletions can't evict the small-file undo history).
# Deliberately much smaller than OTODOCK_MAX_FILE_MB; raise per-install if you
# prefer undo protection for large files over bin disk usage.
# RECOVER_BIN_MAX_MB=100
